import RequestProject.Epistemic.Authority
import RequestProject.Epistemic.Tetralemma

/-!
# Evidence, obligation and authority are three coordinates

A claim being *supported* is not the same as its technical obligations being
*discharged*, and neither of those opens an *authority gate*.  Systems that
fold the three into one "is this claim good?" flag end up either promoting
unauthorised claims or treating an authority decision as if it were evidence.

This module keeps the three apart on one carrier, `GovernedClaim`, reusing the
existing machinery rather than introducing a second authority algebra: evidence
is an `Epistemic.SupportSquare`, and the gate is the diachronic mandate history
of `Epistemic.Authority`.

The separations, each stated as "no function computes one coordinate from
another":

* `no_obligations_from_evidence` — support does not discharge obligations;
* `no_promotion_from_evidence` — evidence does not open the gate;
* `no_promotion_from_obligations` — discharging obligations does not open it
  either.

The gate is what promotion actually reads (`promoted_eq_of_mandate_eq`), and it
is closed unless a principal has granted and not revoked
(`support_and_discharge_cannot_open_closed_gate`,
`conflict_cannot_open_closed_gate`).  The converse warning is
`authority_alone_promotes`: a granted mandate promotes a claim whose obligations
are wide open, which is precisely why the authority coordinate must be visible
rather than inferred.
-/

